"You have to eat to stay alive. So does a dog, a bird, and a fish. But a tree stands in one place for a hundred years and never eats a thing. All the same, it grows. A seed that weighs less than a coin becomes a trunk too heavy to lift. Where does all that wood come from? For a long time people thought the answer was the soil. About four hundred years ago, a scientist named Jan van Helmont tested the idea. He planted a small willow tree in a pot of soil and gave it nothing but water for five years. The tree gained about 74 kilograms. The soil had lost only 57 grams. So the tree was not made of soil. The real answer is in the leaves. A leaf takes in a gas called carbon dioxide from the air, through tiny holes on its underside. Water comes up from the roots. Inside the leaf there are small green parts called chloroplasts. They catch the energy of sunlight and use it to join the carbon dioxide and the water into sugar. This is called photosynthesis, which means putting together with light. Sugar is the plant's food. It burns some for energy, and it builds the rest into leaves, roots, and wood. So most of a tree is made from air and water, joined together by sunlight. Something is left over when sugar is made: oxygen. The leaf lets it out through the same tiny holes, and it mixes into the air. That oxygen comes from the water, not from the carbon dioxide. It is the gas that you, and nearly every other animal, must breathe to stay alive. Now think about your last meal. Rice, bread, and vegetables are parts of plants. Meat and eggs come from animals that ate plants. Follow any food back far enough, and you arrive at a green leaf in the sun. In a way, you run on sunlight too.": "b4d2c2c454f9",
